  "result": "I wrote the rule and its witnesses for `LVMC_11_20_060_o1`, and the gate passed them (`OK`) on the first run, using 1 of the 6 runs.\n\n**The rule**\n- **Who it covers:** the subject must be a vehicle under `DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle`.\n- **When it applies:** the area is a business or residence district (the declared assumption), and the subject starts occupying a sidewalk at this observation while also on a driveway/alley entrance or an alley.\n- **What it requires:** for every sidewalk the subject drives onto this way, it must have come to a complete halt while in the stop zone. The stop zone means off the sidewalk and within `p.stop_point_tolerance` of it. The halt must fall in the unbroken run of stop-zone observations just before the entry.\n\n**One choice I made:** the blueprint writes the search as \"(in the stop zone, or the entry observation itself) since (in the stop zone and halted)\". I wrote it instead as that search's value at the observation just before the entry. The two agree except in one edge case: if the subject was on the sidewalk for a single observation earlier, the blueprint's literal form would let the search run back through that earlier entry. The blueprint says the previous entry must end the search, and my form makes sure it does. I declared no narrowings, since this follows the record's own wording. The reasoning is in the rule file's header and in `task_result.md`.\n\n**Witnesses:** the six planned scenarios (`forbid`, `permit`, `idle`, `blind`, `branch_1`, `history_boundary`) each return their planned verdict. I added `branch_2` (violated), the same unhalted entry but from an alley, so the alley half of the \"driveway or alley\" condition is actually tested. Every witness skips the first observation (it starts at t = 1 s), because there is no earlier observation there to show the subject moving onto the sidewalk.\n\nI found nothing wrong with the node or with the halt concept it uses.\n\nFiles are in `.../iter-002/code/LVMC_11_20_060_o1/`:\n- task_result.md",
